PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Most colleagues (1,2,3,4,5,7,8,9,11,12) select E, citing LSO Rule 3.3-3: disclosure permitted if lawyer reasonably believes imminent risk of death/serious harm exists and disclosure is necessary to prevent it. Eric’s gun purchase + explicit “tonight” statement at ex-wife’s apartment supplies imminence/identifiability; names + Ontario addresses are viewed as narrowly tailored. They reject A (imminence met), B (rule is not absolute), C (info not overbroad), D (no duty triggered by police request alone). 

Colleagues 6 & 10 select C: imminence is satisfied but James exceeded the “necessary” limit by also sending children’s identities and Florida vacation-home addresses (unrelated to locating Eric/ex-wife in Ontario that evening). They note the rule’s strict necessity/scope constraint and argue overbreadth voids the exception. Colleague 7 is equivocal but leans E.

Key unresolved tension: precise scope of “necessary” information under 3.3-3 (Florida details appear extraneous). No one cites contrary authority or commentary. My current view: C is stronger because the rule text and commentary emphasize minimal disclosure; extraneous data violates that limit even if the core threat qualifies.}

